---
/** M1 — How AI generates text. (Draft content for review.) */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ModuleHeader from '../../components/ModuleHeader.astro';
import ModuleWorkbench from '../../components/ModuleWorkbench.tsx';
import References from '../../components/References.astro';
import { MODULES } from '../../lib/modules';
import { buildWorkbenchConfig } from '../../lib/module-content';
import '../../styles/workspace.css';

const m = MODULES.find((x) => x.slug === 'how-ai-generates-text')!;
const config = buildWorkbenchConfig(m.num);
---

<Base
  title={m.title}
  description="A plain account of how large language models predict text — and why they invent sources with such confidence."
  maxWidth="none"
>
  <div class="ws-main">
    <ModuleHeader label="Module 1" duration={m.duration!} title="How AI generates text" />
    <section class="ws-body">
      <div class="ws-cols">
        <div class="ws-reading">
          <p class="standfirst">
            It predicts the next word. Understanding that one fact changes how you
            read everything it produces.
          </p>
          <div class="ws-prose">
            <p>
              A large language model does not know things. It predicts — token by
              token — the most likely continuation of the text so far, learned from
              a vast sweep of human writing. That is the whole trick, and it is
              enough to produce prose that is fluent, plausible, and, often, wrong —
              a system, as Bender et&nbsp;al. (2021) put it, for stitching together
              form without any grip on meaning.
            </p>
            <p>
              Fluency is not truth. Because the model optimises for what <em>sounds</em>
              right, it will produce a confident citation, a clean statistic, a tidy
              quotation — whether or not any of them exist. This tendency to
              “hallucinate” (Ji et&nbsp;al., 2023) isn’t a bug it will grow out of;
              it’s the same mechanism that makes the good sentences good.
            </p>
            <p>
              You don’t need the mathematics. You need the disposition: treat every
              factual claim as unverified until you’ve checked it, and teach your
              students to do the same.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="pull-quote">
            <p>“It isn’t lying. It has no notion of true. It is finishing your sentence — very well.”</p>
            <p>The one idea this module is built around.</p>
          </div>
          <References module="M1" />
        </div>
        <ModuleWorkbench client:load config={config} />
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
